This paper investigates the normalized ground state solution for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
  \quad\mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
  \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^4}|u|^2dx=c^2,\quad u\in H^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \end{split}
  \end{align*} under specific constraints and growth conditions for $\beta, \lambda, \mu, f$ and $M$.

Three-body $	au$-decays, which involve semileptonic interactions with light hadrons, can be examined using the hadronic component of the weak current. Studies of these decays have discovered entries in the vector and axial vectors of the weak interaction that don't align with standard model predictions. One possible explanation for these discrepancies is the presence of hadronic currents that contain nonvalence components, which could be confirmed by analyzing form factors at various energy points. We propose the use of a novel tubular region of the $K_{m L}
u_{	au}$ distribution as a means of determining the electromagnetic form factor of the kaon. By identifying the hadronic mass spectrum of the $K_{m S}
u_{	au}$ decay within this region, we can develop a model for the $K_{m S}	au_{m e}
u_{	au}$ decay spectrum, which, combined with the known $	au$ branching fractions and the $K_{m L}/K_{m S}$ mass ratio, allows us to determine the $K_{m S}	au_{m e}
u_{	au}$ branching fraction. This could help resolve the anomalous elements found in the vector and axial vectors of the weak interaction, which were discovered in the hadronic component of the $ho(770)$ and $J/\psi$ decays of the $	au$ lepton.
